MLLM description: The Blue Grosbeak is a medium-sized bird with a robust body and short, thick bill. Its most distinctive feature is its vibrant blue plumage, which covers the head, back, wings, and tail. The underparts are a lighter shade of blue or grayish-blue. A noticeable reddish-brown patch is present on the wing, adding contrast to the overall coloration. The bird's eyes are dark and expressive, and it has a short, slightly curved beak.

The Blue Grosbeak's size can vary, but they generally measure around 7-8 inches in length. They have a plump body and a relatively short tail. Their wings are broad and rounded, and their legs are strong and sturdy.

In terms of behavior, Blue Grosbeaks are primarily seed-eaters, feeding on seeds from various plants, including grasses, weeds, and shrubs. They are often seen perched on branches or on the ground, searching for food. During breeding season, males are more territorial and may engage in displays to attract mates. They build cup-shaped nests in trees or shrubs, and females lay 3-4 eggs.

